1
00:00:17,007 --> 00:00:17,949
Peace and joy.

2
00:00:17,969 --> 00:00:20,172
I trust you had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:20,253 --> 00:00:21,234
I do not say happy.

4
00:00:21,254 --> 00:00:23,458
I hope it has been a very happy day.

5
00:00:24,539 --> 00:00:31,853
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambodasa.

6
00:00:32,594 --> 00:00:40,027
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambodasa.

7
00:00:40,817 --> 00:00:48,487
Namo Tassa Bhagavato Arahato Sammasambuddhasa.

8
00:00:49,167 --> 00:00:55,234
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed noble and fully self-awakened one.

9
00:00:55,253 --> 00:01:10,251
So these past few nights, we've met these various characters and treated mainly with the whole problem of dukkha, you know, suffering, pleasure, all that sort of stuff.

10
00:01:10,906 --> 00:01:17,155
So I thought we would dig a little deeper and go to the first discourse in the book.

11
00:01:17,575 --> 00:01:19,278
It's called The Root of All Things.

12
00:01:19,959 --> 00:01:24,545
And it's one of the most difficult of discourses.

13
00:01:25,527 --> 00:01:39,867
And Bhikkhu Bodhi has written a book on it, The Discourse on the Root of Existence, he's called it, which after this talk, I think it'll take two nights, you can take the study further.

14
00:01:40,268 --> 00:01:43,350
This is really just an introduction to it.

15
00:01:43,911 --> 00:01:53,640
And there are so many things that are spoken of in this discourse that it really is quite difficult to keep it all together.

16
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:59,203
So the first thing is to just see what it is that we're talking about.

17
00:02:01,525 --> 00:02:04,468
So again, the Blessed One is at Ukkata.

18
00:02:04,828 --> 00:02:10,174
So this is again in the northern country of Kosala, just beneath the Himalayas, it seems.

19
00:02:10,306 --> 00:02:15,231
And he's at the Subaga Grove at the root of the Royal Salah tree.

20
00:02:15,252 --> 00:02:17,713
That's a particular tree.

21
00:02:17,734 --> 00:02:24,201
And there's a group of monks and nuns and other people around.

22
00:02:24,461 --> 00:02:28,325
And he says, listen, attend closely to what I shall say.

23
00:02:28,366 --> 00:02:33,231
So now this is the first paragraph.

24
00:02:33,331 --> 00:02:39,739
And this paragraph is repeated with different subjects all the way through the discourse, or at least most of the discourse.

25
00:02:41,002 --> 00:02:55,204
An untaught ordinary person who has no regard for noble ones and is unskilled and undisciplined in their dharma, who has no regard for true men, is an unskilled and undisciplined in their dharma, perceives earth as earth.

26
00:02:55,884 --> 00:03:01,052
Having perceived earth as earth, he conceives himself as earth.

27
00:03:01,533 --> 00:03:04,098
He conceives himself in earth.

28
00:03:04,437 --> 00:03:06,942
He conceives himself apart from earth.

29
00:03:07,562 --> 00:03:11,227
and he conceives himself, he conceives earth to be mine.

30
00:03:13,508 --> 00:03:14,651
And he delights in it.

31
00:03:15,912 --> 00:03:16,513
Why is that?

32
00:03:16,612 --> 00:03:18,754
Because he's not fully understood it, I say.

33
00:03:19,194 --> 00:03:19,496
Okay.

34
00:03:21,098 --> 00:03:25,681
So now this earth is the earth element, so it's part of our body.

35
00:03:25,722 --> 00:03:37,435
So remember, when the Buddha is talking, he makes it plain if he's talking about the world out there, he's talking about the world that we are personally experiencing, our own inner subjective phenomenal world.

36
00:03:38,680 --> 00:03:54,445
And we've been through these basic concepts about the earth, the solidity of our body, the air, the movement, the water, the elasticity and the fire, the warmth or cold.

37
00:03:55,046 --> 00:03:57,411
So that's how this opens.

38
00:03:57,611 --> 00:04:03,320
Now, the first thing is to understand what he means by perception being

39
00:04:03,841 --> 00:04:04,883
uh, perverted.

40
00:04:05,282 --> 00:04:13,574
So these are called the perversions of, uh, perception, , the perversions of perception.

41
00:04:14,817 --> 00:04:31,401
So the first, the first one is that whenever we talk about ourselves or part of our bodies, or even, even when we see something outside the body from our perspective, uh, we see it, we see what is impermanent as permanent.

42
00:04:31,920 --> 00:04:34,223
Now, that doesn't strike true.

43
00:04:34,244 --> 00:04:45,916
I mean, we know that all things change, but here, impermanent, of course, is the idea of it being moment to moment, whereas for us, everything flows.

44
00:04:46,637 --> 00:04:52,605
So even when we go to bed at night, we don't presume that tomorrow we'll be dead.

45
00:04:53,586 --> 00:04:56,250
There's a sort of presumption that life will carry on.

46
00:04:56,589 --> 00:05:01,776
We perceive our lives as continuing, you see, and that's the perversion.

47
00:05:02,362 --> 00:05:08,154
We don't see it as it really is, which is, of course, arising and passing away very momentarily.

48
00:05:11,360 --> 00:05:14,226
And what is painful, we see as pleasurable.

49
00:05:14,588 --> 00:05:17,673
And this is always referring to this indulgence, right?

50
00:05:17,774 --> 00:05:20,379
Not to pleasure itself, but how we relate to it.

51
00:05:20,940 --> 00:05:21,942
That's the perversion.

52
00:05:22,684 --> 00:05:23,966
And as we know,

53
00:05:24,367 --> 00:05:30,855
indulgence, attachment, dependency brings sorrow in its wake.

54
00:05:31,076 --> 00:05:37,882
Yeah, the grief of loss, anxiety that you might lose it, frustration when you don't get it, and so on.

55
00:05:39,125 --> 00:05:42,668
And of course, there's what is not self we perceive as self.

56
00:05:42,689 --> 00:05:54,122
So this is how we see ourselves, how we experience ourselves as a person, as one integral total person.

57
00:05:55,738 --> 00:05:58,762
Whereas we know we're made up of all sorts of stuff.

58
00:06:01,444 --> 00:06:03,887
And finally, the foul as beautiful.

59
00:06:04,427 --> 00:06:06,009
So here you've got to be careful.

60
00:06:06,048 --> 00:06:13,435
We're not talking about beauty as an aesthetic appreciation, but more the body itself.

61
00:06:14,276 --> 00:06:17,040
Remember, beauty is only skin deep.

62
00:06:17,240 --> 00:06:22,185
So if you take the skin off somebody, it isn't particularly nice.

63
00:06:22,891 --> 00:06:30,442
So these are the four perversions that we have.

64
00:06:30,463 --> 00:06:38,454
Now, when we perceive something, this is the moment when we have that first image, the first photocopy in the mind.

65
00:06:39,055 --> 00:06:44,002
And it's from that first photocopy that other photocopies are added.

66
00:06:45,064 --> 00:06:51,615
And this is the process of a perception slowly building up into a concept.

67
00:06:52,625 --> 00:07:03,999
Now, if you take just a very simple thing like an orange, the first time we saw an orange, we would have perceived it as an object, would have tasted it, et cetera, et cetera.

68
00:07:04,600 --> 00:07:13,891
But the more we ate oranges, the more we found out about oranges, we found they come in all sorts of sizes, in all sorts of sweetness and bitterness.

69
00:07:14,391 --> 00:07:18,177
And then we have this grand concept of an orange.

70
00:07:18,442 --> 00:07:24,928
So that word, which is only a sound, of course, other languages have different words for an orange.

71
00:07:25,350 --> 00:07:26,250
That's only a sound.

72
00:07:26,290 --> 00:07:35,480
But for us, it holds our experience of oranges, which includes, of course, marmalade, without which very difficult to live.

73
00:07:38,302 --> 00:07:47,471
OK, so when we're conceiving here, there are three things involved which distort the process of conception.

74
00:07:47,940 --> 00:08:10,759
first of all there's the wrong view the wrong understanding which is included in these uh perversions that we've talked about uh the perversion of of um of uh perception right it's all there as it were within that concept then of course there's the emotional commitment to it

75
00:08:11,127 --> 00:08:24,237
If you think about politics, if you think about religion, people get very attached to their concepts about what life is about, what politics should be about, etc, etc.

76
00:08:24,819 --> 00:08:30,083
That attachment is a real heart commitment.

77
00:08:30,603 --> 00:08:33,706
And of course, people will die for their beliefs.

78
00:08:35,048 --> 00:08:37,570
And then there's mana, which is the conceit.

79
00:08:38,171 --> 00:08:41,033
So this conceit is always three part.

80
00:08:41,366 --> 00:08:45,131
I am better than the rest of everybody else.

81
00:08:45,231 --> 00:08:47,794
I'm worse than, it's an inverted conceit.

82
00:08:48,196 --> 00:08:51,679
And of course, this one which is more subtle, I'm the same as.

83
00:08:52,201 --> 00:08:55,485
So remember this, I am the same as is of a group.

84
00:08:56,346 --> 00:09:02,114
So it's the group which says, we are better than, we are worse than.

85
00:09:03,134 --> 00:09:08,201
So that underlies, as it were, all concepts.

86
00:09:09,700 --> 00:09:19,235
You can see this in the present day discussion and demonstrations all around racism.

87
00:09:19,836 --> 00:09:28,048
So every tribe, every nation always has strangers.

88
00:09:28,109 --> 00:09:30,131
It always has aliens, always has the other.

89
00:09:30,653 --> 00:09:33,638
And there's always special names for those people.

90
00:09:34,399 --> 00:09:36,923
And that perception,

91
00:09:37,442 --> 00:09:46,216
you know, is enforced at the beginning as a simple perception, but then it grows as a concept.

92
00:09:47,018 --> 00:10:05,525
And really, of course, the way to overcome prejudice is, of course, to begin to see the more underlying connection that lies between human beings and that realizing that culture, color of skin and all that sort of stuff is, you know, really quite superficial.

93
00:10:07,312 --> 00:10:09,716
Okay, so we've got these two things now.

94
00:10:10,417 --> 00:10:16,826
We've got the original distortions of perception when we see something, right?

95
00:10:16,846 --> 00:10:33,551
So that includes what is impermanent as permanent, what is painful as pleasant, what is not self as a self, and what is really not very pleasant as beautiful, okay?

96
00:10:33,571 --> 00:10:36,254
And then when we're developing this

97
00:10:36,471 --> 00:11:03,441
conception there is this underlying misunderstanding underlying wrong view there's an attachment to what I believe I know and there's that conceit with the eye so now having done that he points out these four different relationships that we can have with things so these things are anything that we're experiencing

98
00:11:05,210 --> 00:11:18,945
So he says now there are four types of experiences, and I suppose the easiest one would be, let me see, where am I?

99
00:11:21,807 --> 00:11:22,649
Yes, perceive.

100
00:11:22,928 --> 00:11:28,575
See, he's got a whole category of things where this perception is wrong.

101
00:11:28,595 --> 00:11:33,360
So it's to perceive earth as earth, to believe I am the body, shall we say,

102
00:11:33,998 --> 00:11:41,469
To conceive, sorry, to perceive Earth as Earth is to see the body as body, but then there's this relationship.

103
00:11:42,590 --> 00:11:48,859
He perceives Earth as himself, so that's the identity.

104
00:11:49,600 --> 00:11:53,745
He perceives himself in Earth, so there's me, but I'm in the body.

105
00:11:53,926 --> 00:11:56,690
It's two separate things, but I'm in the body.

106
00:11:57,410 --> 00:12:00,816
There's me as separate from the body, you know,

107
00:12:01,048 --> 00:12:06,356
Like, although the body's there, I'm sort of outside it.

108
00:12:07,399 --> 00:12:08,880
And there's me as mine.

109
00:12:08,921 --> 00:12:13,408
There's the body as mine, which is that possessiveness.

110
00:12:14,568 --> 00:12:20,739
And because of this, the person delights in earth, delights in the body.

111
00:12:21,198 --> 00:12:21,519
Okay?

112
00:12:22,081 --> 00:12:25,586
So with that basis, one is always seeking happiness.

113
00:12:26,206 --> 00:12:29,030
And that's the process of attachment.

114
00:12:31,727 --> 00:12:33,870
So you can see that these delusions go deep.

115
00:12:34,211 --> 00:12:36,852
So he says this for the four elements.

116
00:12:38,195 --> 00:12:40,397
And then he talks about beings, right?

117
00:12:40,437 --> 00:12:43,340
Conceives himself in beings, part of beings.

118
00:12:44,019 --> 00:12:51,248
And he conceives himself as being separate from beings, as being within the community of beings.

119
00:12:52,349 --> 00:12:55,893
And he delights and he owns them.

120
00:12:55,913 --> 00:13:00,317
He says, these are, I belong to these beings.

121
00:13:00,634 --> 00:13:02,778
and he delights in being a being.

122
00:13:05,162 --> 00:13:10,830
And then he goes through a whole section of different types of gods, different types of heavenly gods.

123
00:13:12,152 --> 00:13:23,008
And then he goes through the four Arupa Jhāna, the four formless Jhāna states, the four highest Jhāna states.

124
00:13:24,129 --> 00:13:30,438
And then he goes through the senses, so what he perceives the seen as seen.

125
00:13:30,823 --> 00:13:54,418
whatever object we see, we perceive it, that's the first, we've just talked about the orange, and the heard, and then he goes on about the sensed, which includes the smelt, the tasted and the touched, and then there's the cognised, so what is known, and he conceives himself in what is known.

126
00:13:54,684 --> 00:13:56,187
That's more to do with knowledge.

127
00:13:56,248 --> 00:14:04,604
So this whole discourse is really about the perceptual, conceptual process that we have.

128
00:14:05,166 --> 00:14:10,195
When it comes to dukkha, it's all about the heart, the emotion, the attachments.

129
00:14:10,635 --> 00:14:17,149
But here you can see these things are based on the way that we look at things, on the way that we relate to things.

130
00:14:19,592 --> 00:14:27,822
And there's two here which talk about unity, where he's talking about the mind in a state of concentration.

131
00:14:28,241 --> 00:14:38,793
But it's seemingly it has an effect on our philosophy, where we think everything is based on one particular concept.

132
00:14:38,813 --> 00:14:46,022
So, for instance, a monistic religion believing in one God that underrules everything.

133
00:14:46,913 --> 00:14:59,488
And then there's one with diversity, which is the ordinary mind, which leads us to believe that there are more than one thing which subsists underneath our lives and the universe.

134
00:14:59,928 --> 00:15:02,631
So it produces different types of philosophies.

135
00:15:04,934 --> 00:15:08,178
Monism and pluralism, it goes as far as that, you see.

136
00:15:09,221 --> 00:15:15,250
And then there's the perceives all as all, within the all and separate from the all.

137
00:15:15,530 --> 00:15:19,436
And I belong to the all or the all belongs to me.

138
00:15:19,976 --> 00:15:27,206
All these relationships about the way we relate to everything that we experience.

139
00:15:28,227 --> 00:15:38,621
And that seemingly leads us to pantheism, that everything is God and a more sort of romantic idea of being the all, being part of the all.

140
00:15:40,052 --> 00:15:55,390
And then finally there's Nibbāna, but this Nibbāna that he's talking about is the mistaken Nibbānas, thinking that sensual pleasure is Nibbāna, all these beautiful mental states caused by absorptions are Nibbāna.

141
00:15:55,931 --> 00:15:58,594
And so it goes even to that level.

142
00:16:00,917 --> 00:16:06,503
Okay, so this is the, that's the discourse in, shall we say, in a very brief way.

143
00:16:07,203 --> 00:16:09,586
And for those who...

144
00:16:09,870 --> 00:16:15,235
who want to take it deeper, Bhikkhu Bodhi has written a book on it, The Discourse on the Root of Existence.

145
00:16:15,255 --> 00:16:23,844
I've read it years ago, and I put it on my list of books to read again, probably next life.

146
00:16:24,904 --> 00:16:34,094
So, if we go through that again, if you can sort of handle these different lists.

147
00:16:34,934 --> 00:16:37,397
So, the first list is the way we perceive.

148
00:16:38,913 --> 00:17:08,380
impermanent we see what is actually impermanent as permanent right so that's that sense of continuing sense of continuation especially the sense of self what is painful is pleasant so that's the indulgence business we don't see the consequences uh sometimes and the not self as self so this feeling that i am me this sense of self that we have in us something that's that is always continuous a sense of presence

149
00:17:08,916 --> 00:17:38,501
uh which is of course mind made and of course seeing what is it's translated as foul or um something that is not beautiful but we see it as beautiful so this would be something like the human body which as i say you take the skin off it's not very nice at all so those are your four perversions of perception right so that they're always there you see it's only when you reach these paths and fruits

150
00:17:38,751 --> 00:17:50,344
These attainments that these are beginning to be undermined and of course, they've all gone once we've attained full liberation So there's a whole conceptual cleansing As well.

151
00:17:50,805 --> 00:17:53,488
So remember there's this is this twofold things.

152
00:17:53,508 --> 00:18:06,902
There's the heart being cleansed of its angers and and Excitements and indulgences, but there's also the mind The way we think has to be also corrected

153
00:18:09,008 --> 00:18:20,132
And then when we conceive something, when we have a conception, it's always involved with some sort of wrong understanding somewhere, some attachment to it.

154
00:18:20,152 --> 00:18:21,695
So there's the heart's involvement.

155
00:18:22,416 --> 00:18:28,671
And of course, the sense of conceit, better than, more clear than, et cetera, et cetera.

156
00:18:29,511 --> 00:18:46,488
And then finally, when we come to the actual object that we are relating to, and of course, this includes persons when we're including persons, but really it's to do with, in terms of our spiritual awakening, it's to do with ourselves, a sense of identity.

157
00:18:47,169 --> 00:18:51,153
See, I am me, a sense of locality.

158
00:18:51,333 --> 00:18:52,273
I am in me.

159
00:18:52,954 --> 00:18:55,057
I and the me are separate, but I'm in it.

160
00:18:55,938 --> 00:18:58,539
A sense of distance.

161
00:18:58,789 --> 00:19:23,228
um that i i'm in it but i'm not the same at all and the sense of being that i have so you know like when we say i've got a bad knee or something so those are your four relationships so you can see it's multi-layered you know the way that we build up the world around us and once this uh we've got these concepts then you have what this proliferation this papantia

162
00:19:23,730 --> 00:19:24,010
All right.

163
00:19:24,030 --> 00:19:26,654
And that's where the mind creates these wonderful stories.

164
00:19:26,734 --> 00:19:30,721
At best, we have art and philosophy and maths maybe.

165
00:19:32,864 --> 00:19:39,593
So all these all these layers, one on top of the other, and it produces our philosophies.

166
00:19:40,213 --> 00:19:45,701
So, as I say, if you want to go into this more deeply, then Kubota has written the book on it.

167
00:19:46,303 --> 00:19:53,011
But what I shall do is return to this tomorrow and just and just give the finishing paragraphs to it.

168
00:19:53,633 --> 00:20:02,141
where the Buddha talks about the different types of Arahats and himself.

169
00:20:02,701 --> 00:20:07,665
So that's quite a discourse.

170
00:20:09,227 --> 00:20:22,779
It is the hardest one in the book because Bodhi actually advises you not to read this one and to go on to the second one and keep going and then just come back to it at some time when we've collected some of these concepts.

171
00:20:23,214 --> 00:20:36,271
So we have done this stuff before, but here it's all coming together to show us how we build up our concept of the world, our relationship.

172
00:20:36,311 --> 00:20:38,115
It's all about relationship, remember.

173
00:20:38,134 --> 00:20:43,020
It's all about the way we relate both to ourselves and to others.

174
00:20:43,882 --> 00:20:47,727
Ethics.

175
00:20:47,747 --> 00:20:48,327
Very good.

176
00:20:48,848 --> 00:20:52,834
So I can only hope that hasn't created even more confusion.

177
00:20:53,184 --> 00:21:06,449
but has been inspirational and that it will force you to continue your practice until you are liberated sooner rather than later.

178
00:21:06,469 --> 00:21:07,431
So we can do some practice.

